 into manageable parts, relate unknowns to known quantities, and connect the parts into a coherent solution. Choose an approach appropriate to the problem.\nA problem can sometimes be represented as a partial Lain network: known conditions supply inputs, unknown quantities remain open cells, and relationships connect them. You can construct relationships before all information is available, inspect what is already determined, and let later information complete the computation. Consider whether this representation is useful for the problem.\n4. Verify. Check the result against the original question, units, conditions and examples. Check each conclusion's dependencies, what happens when information changes or is withdrawn, and whether unrelated information matters. Qualify estimates and unresolved conclusions. Encouragement is not evidence of correctness. These stages do not require a prose reasoning transcript.\n\n## output-format-cell\nReturn a sequence of complete executable Lain forms beginning with (. No Markdown fences, language labels, prose or JSON envelope. Provider thinking is separate and not admitted. New define forms create child bindings. Existing parent output ports must be connected with ->, not redefined: redefining them shadows the intended port. Each round has an existing text port user-output-1 or user-output-2. Connect a text reply there; it may explain missing information. Optional observation ports observed-total-N, observed-usable-N, observed-margin-N, observed-fulfillment-N accept total items, usable items, usable minus order quantity, and whether the order can be fulfilled, respectively. N is the round number. To expose a computed value use (-> your-value observed-total-1), for example. You may populate any useful observation ports; no port is required for source admission. Do not put numbers in the text port. Do not substitute precomputed answers for Lain computation.\n\n## context-cell\nOne model worker and one lexical child stay alive across the questions. Earlier child bindings remain live and appear in the binding directory. written-code contains actual earlier generated source; history contains actual user messages and available text replies, maintained by reducers. Return new forms for the current ask. The parent provides inputs and output ports, not a question solution.\n\n## language-cell\nLain uses (define name expression), (network [parameters] body), and prefix calls. + - * / >= and str are support-aware TTMS operators. Unknown operands leave dependent computations unknown until information is supplied. (-> value target) connects cells. str joins text and connected values. ttms-premise-closure attaches the support of its second argument to a callable; normal invocation carries that support to results. Generic example: (define demo (ttms-premise-closure (network [x] (+ x 2)) demo-premise)). Calling (demo input) computes with both input and premise support. Closures can be nested. inspect:premises accepts (cell:reference name) and exposes current value and support. Ordinary definitions persist; premise withdrawal controls availability of supported results. Premise activity does not establish truth or confidence.\n\n## metadata-cell\nNames ending in -1 and -2 belong to separate shipments and questions. boxes-N, items-per-box-N, damage-percent-N and order-quantity-N are independently supported input facts; percentage units are 0 through 100. damage-percent-1 is initially unknown. question-N is the premise identifying the corresponding user question and should support conclusions answering it. shared-rule is the shared premise identifying validity of agent-generated mathematical rules; it does not validate the formulas. delivery-note is unrelated metadata. Damage rates can be estimated or inspected as described by the question or supplied status. Do not invent missing facts.\n\n## ask\nA shipment contains 20 boxes of 12 items. We need to fulfill an order for 200 items. The damaged percentage has not been reported yet. What can we determine now, and what remains unresolved about the usable stock and whether we can fulfill the order?\n\n## written-code\n[]\n\n## user-output-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-total-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-usable-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-margin-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-fulfillment-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## boxes-1\n20\n\n## items-per-box-1\n12\n\n## damage-percent-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## order-quantity-1\n200\n\n## question-1\nA shipment contains 20 boxes of 12 items. We need to fulfill an order for 200 items. The damaged percentage has not been reported yet. What can we determine now, and what remains unresolved about the usable stock and whether we can fulfill the order?\n\n## user-output-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-total-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-usable-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-margin-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-fulfillment-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## boxes-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## items-per-box-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## damage-percent-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## order-quantity-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## question-2\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## shared-rule\nValidity premise for generated mathematical rules\n\n## delivery-note\nTuesday\n\n## history\n[{:role \"user\", :content \"A shipment contains 20 boxes of 12 items. We need to fulfill an order for 200 items. The damaged percentage has not been reported yet. What can we determine now, and what remains unresolved about the usable stock and whether we can fulfill the order?\"}]\n\n## Binding directory\n- cell:reference [known]: callable; inspect the binding and language-introduction-cell | reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"39f5f4e0-c623-379f-aa92-7b07399b86d2\"}\n- inspect:premises [known]: callable; inspect the binding and language-introduction-cell | reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uui